  Given the decomposition, the multi-user backend (piece B) is clearly its own project later. The question is whether
  pieces [1mA + C[0m (tasks core + event log) ship and get reviewed before [1mD + E[0m (subscriptions + notification center), or
  whether all four are one deliverable.

  For an app this size — one page, localStorage, no server — A+C+D+E is small enough to be a single coherent spec, and
  splitting it means you don't see a notification until the second cycle. The argument for splitting is that you'd get a
  working task app you can actually use sooner, and the notification design could adapt to what you learn using it.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Should the first spec cover the whole local app, or just the tasks foundation? → Whole local app, one spec [39m
     [38;5;246m(Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Last question before I lay out the design.

  The architectural point that matters more than the framework: I want the [1mdomain core[0m — tasks, the event log,
  subscriptions, and the rules that turn events into notifications — to be plain modules with no UI imports, testable
  without a browser. That's what makes the eventual backend port cheap (the same core runs server-side) and what makes
  the notification rules actually testable. With that boundary in place, the UI layer is thin and the framework choice
  becomes low-stakes and swappable.

  So the real question is just how much tooling you want standing up on day one:
